Description: Do not narrow the native OS version range to the build host's kernel
 Resolving a native Linux target narrows the OS version range to the running
 kernel, and the result is baked into the compiler being built as its own
 builtin target. The same source therefore produces different bytes on hosts
 with different kernels -- six of them, two u64 pairs in .rodata and
 .data.rel.ro, with every section size unchanged -- so the package cannot be
 reproduced by a rebuilder whose kernel differs, and nothing in a .buildinfo
 records the build machine's kernel for them to match.
 .
 A distribution compiler should not carry this either way: what it assumes
 about the host would otherwise depend on whichever machine happened to
 package it, in the same way that leaving ZIG_TARGET_MCPU at "native" made the
 instruction set depend on it.
 .
 defaultVersionRange() has already supplied a host-independent range before
 this point. Upstream falls back to exactly that on any host whose uname
 release string is not valid semver -- every WSL2 machine, for instance -- so
 the resulting state is one the compiler already reaches routinely.
 .
 An environment-variable guard was tried first, so that only the package build
 skipped detection and the shipped compiler kept upstream behaviour. It is not
 practical: resolveTargetQuery() takes no environ map, and the idiomatic
 std.zig.EnvVar.<NAME>.get(environ_map) route would need one threaded through
 six call sites in std (zig.zig, Build.zig x2, Query.zig x3), which would break
 on every upstream release.
